>> title: Breakfast Sausage Patties
>> category: Fried & Crispy
>> tags: pork, sage, breakfast, griddle, make-ahead
>> counters: Diner
>> aka: sausage patties, breakfast sausage, country sausage, sage sausage, sausage links
>> pairs-with: buttermilk-pancakes
>> servings: 8
>> time: 1 hr 30 min
>> slack: unforgiving — raw pork sausage is cooked through every time, and the six minutes in the pan is not a preference
>> step.1: mix the seasoning dry
>> step.2: work in just until it turns tacky
>> step.3: chill 1 hour, so the sage comes forward
>> step.4: shape eight, dimple, fry 6 min

Mix @rubbed sage{2%tsp}(2 g), @dried thyme{1%tsp}(1 g), @kosher salt{2%tsp}(12 g), @coarse black pepper{1%tsp}(2 g), @red pepper flakes{1/2%tsp}(1 g), @light brown sugar{2%tsp}(8 g) and @grated nutmeg{1/4%tsp} together dry, so the salt is evenly through the lot before it meets the meat.

Work @&(~1)sausage seasoning{} and @ice water{2%Tbs}(30 mL) into @coarsely ground pork{2%lb}(900 g; shoulder, 25–30% fat) with your hands just until the mixture turns tacky and holds a print. Past that it goes springy and eats like a hot dog.

Chill @&(~1)sausage meat{} covered ~chill{1%hr}, which is what lets the sage and the salt reach the middle.

Shape @&(~1)rested sausage{} into eight patties a size wider than the biscuit they will sit on, press a dimple into each, and fry in a dry #cast-iron skillet{} over medium heat ~fry{6%min}, turning once. They shrink and dome; the dimple and the extra width are what stop that.
